{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "chain-tog-speciation-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d: the `chain-tog-speciation` RunSpec -- THC running exhaust and the four hydrocarbon species MOVES splits it into: methane (5), NMHC (79), NMOG (80) and TOG (86) -- computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE. It is the eleventh RunSpec this chain reproduces and the first whose chain is more than one rung deep.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES (§23).\n\n* THE CHAIN IS A FIXED POINT. 101 to 7901 to 8001 to 8601, three rungs. Every earlier chained run -- tire wear, brake wear, the nitrogen species, the crankcase processes -- is exactly one rung, so all ten of them could not tell \"the parent's own-rate quantity\" from \"the parent's quantity\". Read one rung deep, NMOG and TOG are not wrong here: they are ABSENT, which is §69's failure and which `run_chainDepthExceeded` now measures from the other side.\n\n* A CHAINED POLLUTANT-PROCESS CAN HAVE TWO PARENTS. TOG is methane PLUS NMOG. No other run in this repository has a fan-in above one, and the derived parent key the chain used to go through would have handed TOG 501 + 8001 = 8501 -- an ID column holding the SUM of what the join matched, which is §70.1's failure at a second site. The chain now joins through `runspecchainedto` itself, which is the edge list, so a fan-in is a fan-in and not a collision.\n\n* A RATIO TABLE CAN BE KEYED BY THE FUEL SUBTYPE. `methaneTHCRatio` and `HCSpeciation` are keyed by (process or pollutant-process, fuel SUBTYPE, regulatory class, model-year range). A rate row carries a fuel TYPE, so the subtype is reached through the county's fuel supply and its formulations -- the same collapse the criteria ratio makes, and the reason the chain ratio gained a county and a month. Neither table has an electricity subtype, which is why the four species blocks are 104 cohorts where THC is 124.\n\n* A SPECIES CAN BE COMPUTED FROM A TALLY THAT IS NOT REPORTED. Ethanol E70/E85 vehicles of model year 2001 and later have their NMOG and VOC speciated from `altTHC` (pollutant 10001), the THC the fuel-effect machinery would have produced under the E10 base fuel's RVP -- while their REPORTED methane and NMHC still come from the ordinary THC. The whole of it is one number: the high-ethanol substitution overwrites fifteen combustion properties of formulation 27002 and deliberately leaves RVP at 7.7, and `altRVP` is the 8.0 it declined to use. The two criteria ratios then share every factor but the exponential, so their quotient is `exp` of the exponent difference -- 0.9934070 here. Without it this run is 20 rows wrong by 73%, and 520 rows right.\n\n540 rows, worst cell 8.135e-06. Reproduced in pandas from the DEFAULT database before the port -- including the high-ethanol substitution and `altRVP` rebuilt from `e10FuelProperties` -- at methane 5.770e-06, NMHC 7.892e-06, NMOG 5.380e-06 and TOG 8.135e-06.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "chain-tog-speciation",
      "hc-speciation",
      "chained",
      "ragged",
      "fixed-point"
    ]
  },
